16. How to transfer EC2 form one region to another region without taking any downtime ?

Migrating an EC2 instance across regions with zero downtime requires designing a process that keeps your application online while traffic is shifted gradually. Since AWS does not support directly moving an EC2 instance between regions, the solution involves creating AMI snapshots, replicating data, and doing blue-green cutover using DNS.

Below is a professional, detailed answer suitable for interviews:

Step-by-Step Approach (Zero Downtime Strategy)
✅ 1. Prepare the Source EC2 Instance
Before migration, ensure that your instance is consistent and stable:
Apply necessary OS updates.
Ensure ports and security groups are documented.
Check application logs for errors.
Reduce ephemeral data stored locally — move logs/app data to persistent storage (EFS, S3, RDS).

2. Create an AMI of the EC2 Instance
Create an Amazon Machine Image (AMI) of the running EC2 instance.
This captures:
OS
Installed software
Application configurations
Why important?
AMI allows you to rebuild the same instance in another region.

3. Copy AMI to Target Region
AWS allows copying AMIs across regions.
Steps:
Go to EC2 ➝ AMIs ➝ Select AMI ➝ Copy AMI.
Choose the destination region.
AMI gets replicated in target region.
Zero downtime consideration
Creating an AMI does not stop your instance. Users continue accessing the application.

4. Handle Storage / Database Replication
To avoid data loss, you must replicate backend storage.
If using EBS volumes
Take EBS snapshot and copy across regions.
Create volume from snapshot in target region and attach to new EC2 instance.
If using RDS
Use Cross-Region Read Replica, then promote as standalone DB.
If using application-level data
Sync frequently changing data using:
AWS DMS (Database Migration Service)
S3 Cross-Region Replication
rsync over VPN/SSH
AWS DataSync
Key point for interview
To avoid downtime, your data layer must be kept in sync until the final cutover.

5. Create EC2 Instance in Destination Region
Use the copied AMI to launch a new EC2 instance with the same:
Instance type
Network configuration
IAM role
Security groups (recreate if needed)
User-data/startup scripts

6. Test the New Instance (Very Important)
Before cutover:
Validate application functionality
Check logs
Validate DB connections
Validate firewall/security group rules
Ensure performance requirements

7. Perform a Blue-Green Deployment Style Cutover
This is the zero downtime step.
Options:
A. Switch DNS using Route 53
Lower TTL to 60 seconds before migration.
When new instance is ready, update A-record / CNAME to the new EC2 public/ELB endpoint.
Traffic gradually shifts to the new region with minimal or no downtime.
B. Use Elastic Load Balancer (Cross-Region) (Advanced)
If app is load-balanced, you can temporarily register both instances.
Remove old instance after verification.
C. Use CloudFront as global entry point
CloudFront distribution points to origin (EC2/ALB).
Change origin to new EC2 region → clients experience zero impact.

8. Final Data Sync (Before Cutover)
Right before DNS switch:
Perform incremental data sync (only delta changes).
If using RDS read replica:
Promote it during cutover.

9. Final Validation & Decommission
After traffic shifts:
Monitor logs, CPU, application performance.
After stability confirmation, terminate old EC2 instance.

