✅ 8. Why S3 is Object Storage, Not Block Storage
Object Storage (S3)
Stores files as objects (data + metadata)
Each object identified by key
Cannot modify part of an object → must re-upload entire object
Highly scalable, distributed
Best for:
Backups
Media
Logs
Big data
ML datasets
Block Storage (EBS/EFS)
Data stored in fixed-size blocks
Supports partial updates
Low-latency IO
Used for:
Databases
Operating systems
Applications requiring POSIX filesystem
Why S3 is Object Storage?
Designed for durability and infinite scaling
Distributed across AZs
Not tied to a server
Objects immutable (no in-place editing)